(1) PISANO PERIOD of 10 -- the user's claim: last digit repeats every 60
    pi(10) = 60   -> CLAIM IS CORRECT
    structure: pi(2)=3, pi(5)=20, lcm = 60

(2) '1001 = three sixties' -- does this check out arithmetically?
    3 x 60 = 180.  1001 != 180.  1001/60 = 16.6833.  NOT three sixties.
    1001 factors: 7 x 11 x 13 = 1001
    Pisano period of 1001 = lcm(pi(7),pi(11),pi(13)) = lcm(16,10,28) = 560
    (direct: pi(1001) = 560)   -- so 1001's Pisano period is 560, not 180

(3) BUT: 1001 IS A PASCAL ENTRY, AND IN ROW 14.
    C(14,4) = 1001
    C(14,10) = 1001
    row 14 of Pascal: [1, 14, 91, 364, 1001, 2002, 3003, 3432, 3003, 2002, 1001, 364, 91, 14, 1]
    -> 1001 = C(14,4) = C(14,10).  14 is THIS REPO'S APEX (LRC(14), lam=1/14).

(4) FIBONACCI FROM SHIFTED PASCAL (shallow diagonals) -- verify
    F(n) = sum_k C(n-1-k, k) for n=1..39: True
    e.g. n=10: [1, 8, 21, 20, 5] sums to 55 = F(10) = 55

(5) WHY PERIODICITY TRANSFERS: Lucas' theorem makes Pascal mod p self-similar,
    so the shallow-diagonal sums inherit a period.  Check ord and periods:
    p=  2: pi(p)=   3   p-1=  1   p+1=  3   pi | p-1? False   pi | 2(p+1)? True
    p=  5: pi(p)=  20   p-1=  4   p+1=  6   pi | p-1? False   pi | 2(p+1)? False
    p=  7: pi(p)=  16   p-1=  6   p+1=  8   pi | p-1? False   pi | 2(p+1)? True
    p= 11: pi(p)=  10   p-1= 10   p+1= 12   pi | p-1? True   pi | 2(p+1)? False
    p= 13: pi(p)=  28   p-1= 12   p+1= 14   pi | p-1? False   pi | 2(p+1)? True

(6) Does 14 / 1001 / 60 cohere? sanity checks:
    C(14,4)=1001=7*11*13 ; 14=2*7 ; ord_1001(10)=6  (10^3 = -1 mod 1001)
    the repo's two extremal families are {1..13} and {1..11,13,24} -- contain 7,11,13
